WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.462
The public conversation around GDID accelerated after a July 2026 Windows Latest report.

00:00:05.462 --> 00:00:12.253
The report focused on a federal complaint involving Peter Stokes, an alleged member of the Scattered Spider hacking group.

00:00:12.253 --> 00:00:22.635
The DOJ press release says the complaint alleges a luxury jewelry retailer was breached in May 2025, data was exfiltrated, and an approximately eight million dollar ransom demand was made.

00:00:22.635 --> 00:00:28.054
Stokes should be described as accused or alleged unless a later court outcome changes that status.

00:00:28.054 --> 00:00:33.930
The complaint is important because it describes a Global Device Identifier in unusually direct language.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:39.154
It says a Microsoft representative described the identifier as persistent and device-level.

00:00:39.154 --> 00:00:43.855
It also says the identifier is designed to uniquely identify a Windows installation.

00:00:43.855 --> 00:00:50.319
That installation may be on a physical device or a virtual machine, across certain Microsoft services and scenarios.

00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:58.611
The complaint further says the identifier remains consistent across Windows operating system updates, while a reinstall is tied to a new unique GDID.

00:00:58.611 --> 00:01:02.856
Investigators allegedly used the GDID as one piece of correlation evidence.

00:01:02.856 --> 00:01:13.760
The complaint states that Microsoft records connected the GDID to an ngrok signup page at a time matching ngrok records, then connected the same GDID to other activity through proxy infrastructure.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:20.615
Investigators then compared IP address activity associated with the device to account activity they attributed to Stokes.

00:01:20.615 --> 00:01:23.945
The lesson is not that one identifier alone proves identity.

00:01:23.945 --> 00:01:35.045
The lesson is that a persistent service-side device identifier can be powerful when combined with timestamps, IP addresses, account records, provider subpoenas, travel records, and other evidence.

00:01:35.045 --> 00:01:40.857
In an investigation, correlation becomes stronger when independent records point to the same timeline.

00:01:40.857 --> 00:01:44.905
For privacy and governance teams, the story revealed a transparency gap.

00:01:44.905 --> 00:01:51.957
Many Windows users had never heard the term GDID, and Microsoft public documentation is sparse. The right response is not panic.

00:01:51.957 --> 00:02:01.620
It is to understand persistence, scope, legal-process exposure, user visibility, and controls so that organizations can explain their Windows telemetry posture honestly.
